<TopicPage slug="subjects/development-studies/foundational/what-is-development">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Development studies begins with a definition fight: what does it mean for a society to
    develop? Money is the easiest answer and the wrongest complete one. The modern synthesis —
    Amartya Sen's capabilities approach — defines development as the expansion of what people can
    be and do, which is why the field measures health, education and security alongside income.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="measuring more than money" widget="equationStep" config={devCfg} />

  <h2>The income–health curve, and its flattenings</h2>
  <p>
    Plot countries and the Preston curve appears: life expectancy rises steeply with income, then
    flattens — the first dollars buy vaccines, sanitation and nutrition; the later ones buy less
    life. The curve's scatter is the interesting part: some countries sit far above it (good public
    health on modest incomes), others far below. Development policy is largely the study of moving
    a country up its curve, not just along it.
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="income and life expectancy — the Preston curve" widget="plotter" config={hdiCfg} />

  <h2>Averages hide people</h2>
  <p>
    National means conceal distribution: a booming GDP can leave a region, a gender or an ethnic
    group untouched. Development studies therefore insists on disaggregation — poverty rates by
    group, not just by nation — and on measures beyond income: the Multidimensional Poverty Index
    counts deprivations in health, schooling and living standards simultaneously.
  </p>

  <h2>The field's founding question</h2>
  <p>
    Why did the industrial revolution leave most of the world behind for two centuries, and why
    have some countries converged since while others have not? Institutions, geography, colonial
    history, trade terms and conflict each carry part of the answer — and each unit of this
    subject takes one of them seriously.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Cambridge IGCSE Development Studies 0453 (published syllabus); Sen, <em>Development as Freedom</em>; UNDP Human Development Reports; Preston (1975).</p>
</TopicPage>
